Localized Ars Magica rules text — dual licensed (MIT form / CC-BY-SA 4.0 text)
==============================================================================

This directory is the one place in the project where two differently licensed
bodies of work sit in the same files, so read this before reusing anything from
it.

The form is MIT
---------------

The JSON *form* is this project's own work and is covered by the MIT License in
the top-level `LICENSE` file:

  - the schema and the object/key names (`name`, `description`, `summary`,
    `specialties`, ...),
  - the stable slug-identifier scheme the text is keyed by
    (`spell.aegis_of_the_hearth`, `virtue.puissant_ability`, `art.creo`, ...),
  - the file layout, the split into one file per rules domain, and the key
    ordering conventions.

So writing your own data against this schema — a translation into a language
this project does not ship, a homebrew catalogue, or tooling that reads and
writes this format — needs only the MIT License.

The text is CC-BY-SA 4.0
------------------------

The *string values* are extracted from the Ars Magica rulebooks and are NOT
MIT-licensed. That covers every `name`, `description`, `summary` and
`specialties` entry under `<lang>/` — the spell, Virtue/Flaw, Ability, Art,
House and equipment text the application displays. Much of it is verbatim
rulebook prose; `en/spells.json` in particular carries full spell descriptions
unchanged. It is derived expressive content and keeps the license of the
Markdown it came from.

A translation of that text is a derivative of it, so a new `<lang>/` directory
filled with translated rules text is CC-BY-SA 4.0 as well — even though the
schema it follows is MIT.

